<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Chiefdom of Maui</h1><p class="st-meta">1500 CE – 1795 CE (approx.) · capital Wailuku</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Hawaiian chiefdom of Maui, which under Kahekili II briefly dominated most of the islands before falling to Kamehameha I.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Around 1500 the chief Piʻilani united the districts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maui">Maui</a> under a single rule, and his reign is remembered as a time of peace and building. Tradition credits him and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kihapiilani">Kihapiʻilani</a> with a paved trail around the island and with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piilanihale">Piʻilanihale</a>, the largest temple platform in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wailuku">Wailuku</a> became a principal royal seat.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maui">Maui's</a> aliʻi nui governed through district chiefs, priests and the kapu system, with irrigated taro in the valleys of west Maui and dryland farming on the slopes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haleakala">Haleakalā</a>. Rulers fought frequent wars with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chiefdom-of-hawaii-island">Chiefdom of Hawaii Island</a> over the Hāna district. In the 18th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kekaulike">Kekaulike</a> strengthened the kingdom, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kahekili-ii">Kahekili II</a>, who ruled from about 1765, conquered the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chiefdom-of-oahu">Chiefdom of Oahu</a> in 1783 and controlled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/molokai">Molokaʻi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lanai">Lānaʻi</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamehameha-i">Kamehameha I</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maui">Maui's</a> forces at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iao-valley">ʻĪao Valley</a> in 1790. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kahekili-ii">Kahekili</a>'s death in 1794 his realm was divided, and in 1795 Kamehameha took Maui on his way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oahu">Oʻahu</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahaina">Lahaina</a> later served as capital of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-hawaii">Kingdom of Hawaii</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="chiefdom, 1500 CE – 1795 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">chiefdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">North America and Oceania</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:89.55%;width:5.87%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1500 CE – 1795 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Wailuku</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Piʻilani, Kekaulike, Kahekili II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hawaiian religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Hawaiian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-states">United States</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Maui</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1648&amp;focus=chiefdom-of-maui" aria-label="Chiefdom of Maui on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="470 1405 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Chiefdom of Maui lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M790 1661L759 1646"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M759 1646h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M790 1661h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M790 1661h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Wailuku. <a href="/atlas?year=1648&amp;focus=chiefdom-of-maui">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

